.TH YR-SCAN 1 "" YARA-X "User Commands"
.SH NAME
yr\-scan \- Scan a file or directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fByr scan\fR [\fB\-C\fR|\fB\-\-compiled\-rules\fR] [\fB\-c\fR|\fB\-\-count\fR] [\fB\-\-cpu\-limit\fR] [\fB\-d\fR|\fB\-\-define\fR] [\fB\-\-disable\-console\-logs\fR] [\fB\-w\fR|\fB\-\-disable\-warnings\fR] [\fB\-\-exclude\fR] [\fB\-f\fR|\fB\-\-fast\-scan\fR] [\fB\-\-ignore\-invalid\-rules\fR] [\fB\-I\fR|\fB\-\-ignore\-module\fR] [\fB\-\-include\-dir\fR] [\fB\-\-max\-matches\-per\-pattern\fR] [\fB\-x\fR|\fB\-\-module\-data\fR] [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-negate\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-mmap\fR] [\fB\-o\fR|\fB\-\-output\-format\fR] [\fB\-\-path\-as\-namespace\fR] [\fB\-m\fR|\fB\-\-print\-meta\fR] [\fB\-e\fR|\fB\-\-print\-namespace\fR] [\fB\-s\fR|\fB\-\-print\-strings\fR] [\fB\-g\fR|\fB\-\-print\-tags\fR] [\fB\-\-profiling\fR] [\fB\-r\fR|\fB\-\-recursive\fR] [\fB\-\-relaxed\-re\-syntax\fR] [\fB\-\-scan\-list\fR] [\fB\-z\fR|\fB\-\-skip\-larger\fR] [\fB\-t\fR|\fB\-\-tag\fR] [\fB\-p\fR|\fB\-\-threads\fR] [\fB\-a\fR|\fB\-\-timeout\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fI[NAMESPACE:]RULES_PATH\fR> <\fITARGET_PATH\fR>
.SH DESCRIPTION
Scan a file or directory
.PP
<RULES_PATH> can be the path to a file containing YARA rules, or the path to a directory
containing *.yar or *.yara files. When <RULES_PATH> is a directory, it will be traversed
recursively. Multiple <RULES_PATH> can be specified.
.PP
Each path can be prefixed with a namespace, the namespace and the path are separated by
a semicolon (`:`), like in `namespace:rules_file.yar`. All rules in the path will be put
under the specified namespace, isolated from rules in other namespaces.
.PP
<TARGET_PATH> is the file or directory that will be scanned.
.PP
Examples:
.PP
.nf
yr scan rules_file.yar scanned_file
yr scan rules_dir scanned_file
yr scan namespace:rules_file.yar scanned_file
yr scan namespace:rules_dir scanned_file
.fi
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-C\fR, \fB\-\-compiled\-rules\fR
Indicate that <RULES_PATH> is a file containing compiled rules

YARA rules can be compiled with the `yr compile` command. The file produced by
this command can be passed later to `yr scan` by using this flag.
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-count\fR
Print only the number of matches per file
.TP
\fB\-\-cpu\-limit\fR \fI<PERCENTAGE>\fR
Limit the CPU usage of the scan (percentage from 1 to 99)
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-define\fR \fI<VAR=VALUE>\fR
Define external variable

Examples:

.nf
\-\-define some_int=1
\-\-define some_float=3.14
\-\-define some_bool=true
\-\-define some_str=\\"foobar\\"
.fi
.TP
\fB\-\-disable\-console\-logs\fR
Disable printing console log messages
.TP
\fB\-w\fR, \fB\-\-disable\-warnings\fR[=\fI<WARNING_ID>...\fR]
Disable warnings

When used alone all warnings are disabled. It can receive a comma\-separated
list of with the names of the warnings to disable.

Examples:

.nf
\-\-disable\-warnings
\-\-disable\-warnings=slow_pattern
\-\-disable\-warnings=slow_rules,redundant_modifier
\-\-disable\-warnings=slow_rules \-\-disable\-warnings=redundant_modifier
.fi
.TP
\fB\-\-exclude\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Exclude a file or directory from scanning

May be specified more than once. Directory paths exclude the entire subtree
without traversing it. Also applies to single\-file targets and `\-\-scan\-list`
entries. Relative paths are resolved against the current working directory, not
<TARGET_PATH>.

Paths are literal, not glob patterns. Quote shell wildcard characters.
Symbolic links in parent directories are resolved when matching paths (for
example, if `link` points to `real`, excluding `link/dir` also excludes
`real/dir`). The excluded file or directory itself is never opened. On Windows,
matching is case\-insensitive. Exclusions affect scan targets, not rule
compilation.

Examples:

.nf
yr scan \-\-recursive \-\-exclude ./files/private rules.yar ./files
yr scan \-\-exclude ./files/excluded.bin \-\-exclude ./files/private rules.yar ./files
.fi
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-fast\-scan\fR
Enable fast\-scan mode

In fast\-scan mode, scanning is optimized for speed by avoiding tracking multiple
matches for patterns when they are only used in simple boolean checks. The scanner
stops tracking matches for a pattern as soon as the first match is found, provided
that the pattern is not used in any context that requires knowing all occurrences
(like #a, @a, !a).

As a consequence, options like `\-\-print\-strings` won\*(Aqt output all matching patterns,
but only the first occurrence of some of the patterns.
.TP
\fB\-\-ignore\-invalid\-rules\fR
Ignore rules that fail to compile and continue with the valid ones
.TP
\fB\-I\fR, \fB\-\-ignore\-module\fR \fI<MODULE>\fR
Ignore rules that use the specified module

Rules that use the specified module will be ignored, as well as any rules that
depends directly or indirectly on such rules.

This option can be used more than once for ignored different modules.
.TP
\fB\-\-include\-dir\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Directory in which to search for included files

If not given, the current working directory is used. May be specified multiple
times; directories will be searched in order.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-matches\-per\-pattern\fR \fI<MATCHES>\fR
Maximum number of matches per pattern

When some pattern reaches the maximum number of occurrences it won\*(Aqt produce
more matches. This can affect rules that rely on the number of occurrences of
some pattern. For instance, the expression `#a > 100` will be false if this
limit is set to 100 or less.
.TP
\fB\-x\fR, \fB\-\-module\-data\fR \fI<MODULE=FILE>\fR
Pass FILE\*(Aqs content as extra data to MODULE

Some modules require supplementary data to work, in addition to the scanned
file. This option allows you to provide that extra data. The flag can be used
multiple times to supply data to different modules. The content of the FILE is
loaded and interpreted by the respective module.

Examples:

.nf
\-\-module\-data=mymodule0=./example0.json \-\-module\-data=mymodule1=./example1.json
.fi

In this example, the contents of example0.json and example1.json will be passed
to mymodule0 and mymodule1, respectively.
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-negate\fR
Print non\-satisfied rules only
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-mmap\fR
Don\*(Aqt use memory\-mapped files

By default, large files are memory\-mapped as this is typically faster than
copying file contents into memory. However, this approach has a drawback: if
another process truncates the file during scanning, a `SIGBUS` signal may
occur and the YARA\-X process will crash.

This option disables memory mapping and forces the scanner to always read files
into an in\-memory buffer instead.
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\-format\fR \fI<FORMAT>\fR
Output format

The format in which results will be displayed. Any errors or warnings will not
be in this format, only results.

Examples:

.nf
\-\-output\-format=ndjson
.fi
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
text: Default output format
.IP \(bu 2
ndjson: Newline delimited JSON (i.e: one JSON object per line)
.IP \(bu 2
json: JSON output (i.e: one JSON object for all results, only printed out at the end)
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-path\-as\-namespace\fR
Use file path as rule namespace
.TP
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-print\-meta\fR
Print rule metadata
.TP
\fB\-e\fR, \fB\-\-print\-namespace\fR
Print rule namespace
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-print\-strings\fR[=\fI<N>\fR]
Print matching patterns

The printed patterns can be optionally limited to <N> characters. By default
they are limited to 120 characters.

Examples:

.nf
\-\-print\-strings
\-\-print\-strings=50
.fi
.TP
\fB\-g\fR, \fB\-\-print\-tags\fR
Print rule tags
.TP
\fB\-\-profiling\fR
Show profiling information
.TP
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-recursive\fR[=\fI<MAX_DEPTH>\fR]
Scan directories recursively

When <TARGET_PATH> is a directory, this option enables recursive scanning
of its contents. You can optionally specify a <MAX_DEPTH> to limit how deep
the scan goes:

.nf
\-\-recursive     scan nested subdirectories with no depth limit.
\-\-recursive=0   scan only the files in <TARGET_PATH> (no subdirectories)
\-\-recursive=3   scan up to 3 levels deep, including nested subdirectories
.fi

If \-\-recursive is not specified, the default behavior is \-\-recursive=0.

Examples:

.nf
\-\-recursive
\-\-recursive=3
.fi
.TP
\fB\-\-relaxed\-re\-syntax\fR
Use a more relaxed syntax check while parsing regular expressions
.TP
\fB\-\-scan\-list\fR
Indicate that TARGET_PATH is a file containing the paths to be scanned

<TARGET_PATH> must be a text file containing one path per line, or `\-` to read
paths from standard input until EOF. The paths must be either absolute paths,
or relative to the current directory.
.TP
\fB\-z\fR, \fB\-\-skip\-larger\fR \fI<FILE_SIZE>\fR
Skip files larger than the given size
.TP
\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-tag\fR \fI<TAG>\fR
Print only rules tagged as TAG
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-threads\fR \fI<NUM_THREADS>\fR
Use the specified number of threads

The default value is automatically determined based on the number of CPU cores.
.TP
\fB\-a\fR, \fB\-\-timeout\fR \fI<SECONDS>\fR
Abort scanning after the given number of seconds
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fI[NAMESPACE:]RULES_PATH\fR>
Path to a YARA source file or directory (optionally prefixed with a namespace)
.TP
<\fITARGET_PATH\fR>
Path to the file or directory that will be scanned
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fByr\fR(1)
